<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Radegund</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 520–587</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Saint Radegund, Radegunda · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merovingian-dynasty">Merovingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Radegund was a Thuringian princess captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> in 531 and married to the Merovingian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Chlothar I</a>. She left him around 550, became a nun and founded the Abbey of the Holy Cross at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, which received a relic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. She was venerated as a saint soon after her death in 587.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Radegund was born about 520 to Bertachar, one of the three kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>. Her uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herminafrid">Hermanfrid</a> killed Bertachar in battle and took her into his household. In 531 the Frankish king Theuderic and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-i">Chlothar I</a> defeated Hermanfrid and conquered his kingdom, and Chlothar took Radegund back to Merovingian Gaul. He sent the child to his villa of Athies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picardy">Picardy</a> for several years and married her in 540.</p><p>She was one of six wives or concubines of Chlothar and had no children with him. She was noted for her almsgiving. By 545 her brother was the last surviving male of the Thuringian royal family, and Chlothar had him murdered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Radegund fled the court and sought the protection of the Church. She persuaded Medardus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Noyon, to ordain her a deaconess, and around 560 she founded the monastery of Sainte-Croix in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, where she cared for the sick. She was widely believed to have the gift of healing. The nuns lived under the Rule for Virgins of Caesarius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a>, which she had received from her niece Caesaria. They had to read and write, to spend several hours a day reading scripture and copying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, and to weave and sew. The Rule enclosed them strictly, so that they could not attend Radegund's funeral.</p><p>The abbey took its name from a relic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a> that Radegund obtained from the Byzantine Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justin-ii">Justin II</a>. Maroveus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, refused to install it, and at her request King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigebert-i">Sigebert I</a> sent Eufronius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> to perform the ceremony. For the occasion <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venantius-fortunatus">Venantius Fortunatus</a> composed hymns, among them Vexilla Regis, still sung on Good Friday and Palm Sunday. Radegund was known as an extreme ascetic, who ate only legumes and green vegetables and refused wine, mead and beer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Radegund died on 13 August 587. Her friends <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venantius-fortunatus">Venantius Fortunatus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a> both wrote about her and attended her funeral three days later. She was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, in what became the church of Saint Radegonde, where her tomb remains in the crypt and the centre of devotion to her. The nun Baudonivia wrote another life of her. After her death the nuns of the abbey rebelled for two years against an abbess appointed by the male hierarchy.</p><p>She is patron saint of several churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and of Jesus College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, built on the site of a priory dedicated to Saint Mary and Saint Radegund. Many places in France are named Sainte-Radegonde, and two Austrian municipalities are named after her.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Radegund” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He shared in the conquest of the Kingdom of the Burgundians and of Thuringia, where he took the princess <b>Radegund</b> as his wife.</blockquote><a href="/state/frankish-kingdom-of-soissons">Frankish Kingdom of Soissons</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.98,11.03&amp;year=563" aria-label="Erfurt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4801 396 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Radegund"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5251 666h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5251 666h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.98,11.03&amp;year=563">What was here in 563 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
